# Events

```js
_KDB.events.emit(EVENTS.CHANGED, payload);
var unsubscribe = _KDB.events.subscribe(EVENTS.CHANGED, handler, { signal: scope.signal });
```

`emit` calls handlers synchronously and isolates them: a throwing handler is logged and does not stop the others. The bus is one-way: no callbacks or acknowledgements in payloads.

## Which mechanism

| Intent | Use |
|---|---|
| Command to a known owner | direct call: `_KDB.api.<owner>.dispatch(...)` or `_KDB.services.<service>.<method>(...)` (return value, stack trace, awaitable) |
| Tell others something happened | fact-event emitted by the owner |
| Command whose handler must stay unknown | request-event (rare) |

A request-event is allowed only when the handler is genuinely polymorphic, the caller must not depend on the owner (for example `core` asking `dashboard`), or several owners must each react. Never use events to avoid an import or as a hidden RPC channel.

## Fact-events

- Emitted only by the owner, after the change is applied and recorded in its state.
- Only when another module actually reacts; no "just in case" events.
- Subscribers change only their own state and call other modules through their APIs.

## Naming

| Type | Pattern | Example |
|---|---|---|
| Fact about an entity | `<region>:<entity><VerbPast>` | `tableData:loadFinished`, `canvas:dropValidated` |
| Fact about the region | `<region>:<verbPast>` | `tabs:changed`, `authoring:entered` |
| Request | `<region>:<verb><Entity>Requested` | `header:toggleRequested` |

Event names are part of the frozen contract: do not rename existing ones.

## Constants and catalog

- Each name has one constant in the emitter's home: the module's `events.js` (template) or, in existing modules, `<module>/events/eventNames.js`. Shared families live in `core/events/<region>.js`.
- Subscribers import the constant; no string literals at emit or subscribe sites.
- Payloads are JSON-serialisable: no functions, DOM nodes, jQuery or Cognos objects.
- Subscriptions use `{ signal }` or store the unsubscribe function in `_KDB.runtime.<owner>.subscriptions` (see `runtime.mdc`).
- New or changed events are recorded in `architecture/eventSubscribe.md` (name, fact/request, emitter, subscribers, payload).
